module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_designed_narrowing_stops_inhabiting_the_subject_it_narrowed

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_designed_narrowing_stops_inhabiting_the_subject_it_narrowed: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_designed_narrowing_stops_inhabiting_the_subject_it_narrowed" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**A FIXTURE THAT ENUMERATES HAND-PICKED INPUTS INSTEAD OF DERIVING THEM CARRIES A HIDDEN PREMISE: THAT EACH PICK STILL INHABITS THE SUBJECT'S CONSTRAINTS.** The narrowing is normally the RIGHT move -- a claim whose discriminator is the runtime does not need the whole candidate field, and a smaller field is cheaper and says exactly as much. What the narrowing also does, invisibly, is freeze a set of inputs against the constraint population that existed when they were chosen. When a later change TIGHTENS that population, the picks can stop satisfying it, and the claim keeps passing: it reaches its expected verdict through the NEW exclusion rather than through the mechanism it exists to exercise. Nothing reds. The assertion is unchanged, the subject is correct, the verdict is correct, and the reason is different.",

    "SPECIMEN ONE (2026-09-14, gunbc#11193 merging #11308). `v4_1_input_for_runtime` had been narrowed, deliberately and with a measurement behind it, from the twenty topologies `fleet_admitted_topologies` yielded to TWO designed shapes -- tp4 x 2 replicas and tp8 x 1 -- because the claim's discriminator is the runtime and not the field, and because the unnarrowed claim cost 393,216 eval_steps and 508ms CPU against a 500ms ceiling. The annotation stated what the narrowing could not lose and was right about it. BOTH SHAPES CONSUME EIGHT RANKS. #11308 then replaced a host-count scalar with a subject-relative admissible-host SET and made the host fit a HARD CONSTRAINT, and V4.1's subject holds ONE fabric group. So both picks became unplaceable, the field would have been emptied on host fit before Pareto, and the claim would have kept returning its expected needs-evidence verdict by a route that never consults runtime authorization at all.",

    "WHAT MADE IT VISIBLE WAS A MERGE CONFLICT, WHICH IS THE PART TO DISTRUST. The two changes touched the same argument, so git refused and the author had to look. Had #11308 edited a different line -- or had the narrowing lived in a second file -- the merge would have been CLEAN and the fixture would have survived intact and wrong. So the discovery path here was luck about textual adjacency, and a class whose detection depends on that is not detected.",

    "AND THE AUTHOR WAS ALREADY WATCHING THE ADJACENT COMPONENT. The same session had been warned, that hour, against substituting one component of a composite decision identity and assuming the rest travelled with it, and had verified exactly that: which FLEET SNAPSHOT the field should be built from, leaning on a construction another passing claim proved. The component it did not check was the one it had chosen ITSELF and therefore did not experience as an input -- the topologies. A pick you authored does not present itself as an assumption.",

    "SPECIMEN TWO, FOUND BY AN INDEPENDENT READER WITHIN THE HOUR OF SPECIMEN ONE BEING FILED, and that provenance is why it is worth recording rather than being folded in as a variant. Resolving the same merge, the author kept their own side of an import conflict on the stated ground that it was THE SUPERSET -- `import gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed { FabricGroup, FabricGroupA, FabricGroupB }` over the incoming `{ FabricGroupA }`. It had been the superset, of usage that the merge REPLACED. After the merge, `FabricGroup` and `FabricGroupB` occurred in that module only inside a string literal and an annotation, and a reviewer reported the unused pair as a non-finding. The author had spent that hour checking whether one identity component travelled across the merge; the component they did not check was the import list they had themselves chosen to preserve.",

    "AND IT IS NOT COSMETIC, WHICH IS WHAT JOINS THE TWO SPECIMENS INTO ONE CLASS RATHER THAN TWO. A braced import binds those names into the module's candidate sets, and an over-wide binding is the same mechanism as the `AmbiguousBareNameRead` refusal the corpus was being repaired for that same morning -- a name reachable from more than one declaration with nothing saying which is meant. So a narrowing that stops inhabiting its subject and an import list that stops inhabiting its module are the same failure at two grains: a hand-authored set, correct when written, preserved across a change that moved what it was correct about. NOTE THE ASYMMETRY IN WHO FINDS THEM: specimen one was surfaced by a merge conflict and specimen two by a reader, and NEITHER was surfaced by a gate.",

    "SPECIMEN THREE, AT FIELD GRAIN RATHER THAN FIXTURE GRAIN, WHICH IS WHERE THIS CLASS IS EXPENSIVE. The first two specimens are hand-picked INPUTS that stopped inhabiting their subject. The same failure occurs one level up, in what a claim ASSERTS: four claims in gunbc#11193 expected `ServingDeploymentNeedsEvidence` from the live V4.1 serving decision, and every one of them was authored while that decision's domain was generated over an EIGHT-host scalar. gunbc#11308 replaced the scalar with the subject's admissible host SET, V4.1's subject holds one fabric group, and tp5 through tp8 correctly ceased to exist -- taking with them every shape whose per-rank Engram share was small enough to fit. All four then returned `NoFeasibleServingDeployment`, correctly, and were red.",

    "THE ASYMMETRY THAT MAKES FIELD GRAIN WORSE THAN FIXTURE GRAIN: a hand-picked fixture input is visible in the claim that uses it, so a reader editing that claim can see what was frozen. AN EXPECTED RESULT FREEZES THE WHOLE SUBJECT and names none of it -- `ServingDeploymentNeedsEvidence` mentions no host count, no topology and no memory figure, so nothing in the claim's text records that it was true only over an eight-host domain. Two of these four ran on a LIVE input nobody had edited, which is the tell: the author had changed a neighbouring fixture and reasonably suspected their own edit, while the claims that had never been touched were failing for the same reason.",

    "AND THE DIAGNOSIS COST THREE WRONG ANSWERS BEFORE EXECUTION SETTLED IT, each self-consistent and each reached by READING rather than RUNNING. (1) The field was assumed emptied by the per-rank memory screen; the first probe showed the cause named the parent route constraint instead. (2) That was then read as the memory axis NOT being involved; it fires INSIDE the route decision, so both readings were half right and the conclusion drawn from each was wrong. (3) A budget fold returning zero for an UNESTABLISHED budget was identified as a live defect -- an unknown becoming a zero hard bound that excludes every candidate -- and was one step from being reported; the probe showed the budget is ESTABLISHED at every topology and that arm never fires. The supporting quotation for that non-existent defect would have been the author's OWN ANNOTATION calling the zero unreachable. A code reading pointed one way and execution the other three times in one afternoon, and execution was right three times.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE: for every hand-enumerated fixture input, name the constraint population it must satisfy, and ask whether the change in front of you ADDS OR TIGHTENS a member of that population.** If it does, the picks are re-validated by EXECUTION and not by inspection -- a hard constraint's exclusion is invisible in a verdict that was already the expected one. Two mechanical tells: an annotation defending a narrowing by citing a figure or a domain size (\"rather than the twenty ...\", \"eight hosts\"), which dates the pick to a population that can move; and a claim whose expected result is a REFUSAL or a needs-evidence arm, because those are reachable by more than one mechanism and so cannot distinguish which one fired.",

    "DISTINCT FROM ITS NEIGHBOURS, and the distinctions are the reason for a separate row. `a_fixture_at_an_absorbing_extreme_erases_the_test` is about a pick chosen AT a degenerate value, which is wrong when authored; these picks were correct when authored and were falsified later by someone else's change. `a_positive_control_certifies_the_defect_it_exists_to_catch` is a control agreeing with a WRONG ROW; here the row, the control and the verdict are all right, and only the path is wrong. `verdict_stale_at_the_merge_instant` is a VERDICT computed over the wrong base -- an evidence-to-tree mismatch resolved by re-running; re-running this fixture does not help, because it passes.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and the containment was a textual merge conflict rather than any mechanism. The compiler accepted both states, the claim was green in the narrowed form and would have been green in the falsified one, and no gate distinguishes them.",

    "CEILING: mechanically preventable, and the derivation is why it is not higher. Whether a fixture's enumerated input satisfies the hard constraints its subject's decision carries is DECIDABLE -- the constraint population and the candidate are both modeled values, so a pass can evaluate every hard constraint against every hand-supplied candidate and refuse a fixture whose field is emptied by a constraint the claim does not name. What construction cannot reach is the author's INTENT about which mechanism the claim exists to exercise: that a needs-evidence verdict was meant to come from runtime authorization rather than host fit is not carried by any type today, so the wall must compare a declared expected mechanism against the one that fired, which is validation.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: a claim may DECLARE the constraint or axis its verdict is expected to turn on, and the selection path reports WHICH constraint actually excluded each candidate, so a fixture whose field is emptied by an undeclared constraint refuses. That is not satisfied by a richer refusal string, and it is not satisfied by the exclusion identities the spine already carries -- it needs the claim side to state its expected mechanism, which nothing in the witness vocabulary can express today. SUFFICIENT FOR: a narrowed field that stops inhabiting a newly tightened constraint reds at the claim rather than passing for a new reason.",

    "WHY IT RANKS: the artifact it corrupts is a deliberately narrowed fixture, which is exactly the artifact a cost ruling produces. So the class is CREATED BY GOOD PRACTICE -- every claim split to clear a ceiling mints a new instance -- and it compounds with the cost pressure that motivated the narrowing, because the cheaper a field is the more it has been hand-chosen and the more premises it has frozen. STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS UNCOMFORTABLE: this programme spent the two days before the specimen narrowing claims under an operator ruling to break them below a cost ceiling, so it was MANUFACTURING instances of this class throughout, and the specimen is one of its own products rather than an accident that wandered in. Every narrowing landed in that campaign is a candidate instance, and none of them was re-checked against a constraint population that has since moved.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
